 :version-caveat
 "CAREFUL. This repo's deps.edn :kotoba alias pins amu at 3579cfd, and at that
  revision frontend.cljc still lived INSIDE amu; by 02c7e57 it has moved out to
  kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema. So `kbb -M:kotoba` and `bin/amu` here are not
  only two entrypoints, they were two different FRONTEND VERSIONS. Any
  nbb-versus-JVM difference measured that way is confounded, and the entries
  below say which amu answered. Where an uncoupled comparison was run, it is
  recorded inside the reproduction it belongs to -- see the
  hetero-vector-with-entry rows and the :confirmed note under
  :wasm32-internal-error-on-hetero-vector-at."
 :kotoba-kir {:sha "099c627609ad506babf05f2d5e9a73b95c1b026b"
              :note "resolved from amu's deps-lock.edn into ~/.gitlibs"}
 :invocation "node <amu>/bin/amu <command> <ABSOLUTE path>.kotoba [--target T] [--output O]"
 :caveat "Pass bin/amu an ABSOLUTE source path. A relative one answers
          :kotoba/invalid-data \"input could not be read\", which reads like a
          grammar rejection and is not one."

 :deliberately-not-advancing-the-amu-pin
 "This repo's deps.edn :kotoba alias is LEFT at amu 3579cfd on purpose, not by
  neglect. Advancing it would move `kbb -M:kotoba` onto the same frontend
  bin/amu uses, which is the frontend that crashes on `hetero-vector-at` --
  and the three conformance roots this repo already compiles that way
  (expgolomb, sps, ebsp) all read their results with hetero-vector-at.

  Stated precisely, because the tempting version of this sentence is stronger
  than the measurement: the JVM path at 02c7e57 DOES compile a
  hetero-vector-at file (hetero-vector-with-entry.kotoba, exit 0), so
  advancing the pin is not known to break the conformance step. What is not
  known is whether it still handles the :require + --source-path shape those
  three roots actually use -- that probe died for an unrelated classpath
  reason and is recorded under :unverified. So the pin is left alone because
  moving it would be an unmeasured change to a working step, not because
  moving it is known to break one. The kernels added alongside this note do
  not go through that alias at all; they are compiled by bin/amu directly, so
  they are unaffected either way."

 :environment-note
 "Measured 2026-08-30 10:16 local, mid-session: the shared amu checkout's
  node_modules became a symlink to ITSELF
  (node_modules -> .../amu/node_modules), which makes bin/amu die with
  \"Cannot find module .../node_modules/nbb/cli.js\" because it resolves its
  nbb entrypoint through that path. Not caused by this work and not repaired
  by it -- another session owns that checkout. The final runs recorded here
  used a private copy of the SAME amu revision (src/bin/scripts/tools/runtime
  copied, nbb 1.5.211 supplied from another checkout's node_modules), so the
  compiler code is identical and only its location differs."

