 :reproductions
 [{:id :no-lowering-for-shift-and-friends
   :file "shift-heads.kotoba"
   :command ["check"]
   :exit 65
   :diagnostic {:format :kotoba.cli-error/v1 :ok false :error :subset
                :code :kotoba.error/subset-reject :severity :error
                :message "operation has no admitted lowering"}
   :rejected #{bit-shift-right bit-shift-left unsigned-bit-shift-right
               mod rem min max /}
   :admitted #{quot bit-and bit-or bit-xor inc dec cond case ->}
   :authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang lang/guest-grammar.edn :admitted-builtins
               lists ALL EIGHT of the rejected names alongside the admitted
               ones. So this is drift between the authority and the frontend,
               not a documented restriction."
   :impact "H.264 is specified in terms of arithmetic right shifts. Every
            shift in src/h264/{quant,transform}.kotoba is spelled as floor
            division or as multiplication by a power of two because of this.
            Both spellings are exact, but they are circumlocutions and the
            source says so, so that nobody 'simplifies' them into `quot` --
            which truncates toward zero and would change every negative
            residual sample by one."
   :status :worked-around}

  {:id :max-parameters-5
   :file "arity-six.kotoba"
   :command ["check"]
   :exit 65
   :diagnostic {:code :kotoba.error/max-parameters
                :message "function parameters exceed ABI-supported arity"}
   :authority "guest-grammar.edn :admission-limits {:max-parameters 5}"
   :defect? false
   :impact "This is the constraint that decided the SHAPE of this repo's
            kernel. Sixteen coefficients cannot cross an exported boundary as
            parameters, so the exported unit is H.264's 1-D butterfly (four
            coefficients plus an output index) and the host composes the 2-D
            transform out of eight calls."
   :status :designed-around}

  {:id :native-rejects-vector-parameter-accepts-record
   :files ["vector-export-param.kotoba" "scalar-record-export-param.kotoba"
           "native-sixteen-field-record.kotoba"]
   :measured
   [{:file "vector-export-param.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 0 :wasm-imports 45}
    {:file "vector-export-param.kotoba" :target "wasm32-browser" :exit 0}
    {:file "vector-export-param.kotoba" :target "aarch64" :exit 70
     :code :kotoba/target-rejected
     :message "typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target,
               typed Wasm target, or qualified native
               string/scalar-record/option-i64/result-i64 features"}
    {:file "vector-export-param.kotoba" :target "x86_64" :exit 70
     :code :kotoba/target-rejected}
    {:file "scalar-record-export-param.kotoba" :target "aarch64" :exit 0}
    {:file "scalar-record-export-param.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 0
     :wasm-imports 45}
    {:file "native-sixteen-field-record.kotoba" :target "aarch64" :exit 0}
    {:file "native-sixteen-field-record.kotoba" :command ["extract-native" "--symbol" "pick"]
     :exit 0 :offset 0 :length 1224 :arity 2}]
   :finding "The native backends reject a :vector-i64 parameter and accept a
             SIXTEEN-FIELD :i64 record in the same position, at arity 2. So a
             whole 4x4 coefficient block CAN reach a native export today. What
             it costs is the Wasm side: any typed value makes the emitted
             module import the 45-function kotoba:typed surface, where the
             scalar kernels emit a module with ZERO imports. That trade, not
             the parameter limit, is the next real decision for widening this
             kernel to the 2-D transform in one call."
   :status :measured-not-yet-exploited}

  {:id :wasm32-internal-error-on-hetero-vector-at
   :file "wasm32-hetero-vector.kotoba"
   :counterexample "hetero-vector-counterexample.kotoba"
   :measured
   [{:file "wasm32-hetero-vector.kotoba" :command ["check"] :exit 0}
    {:file "wasm32-hetero-vector.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 70
     :diagnostic {:format :kotoba.cli-error/v1 :ok false :error :internal
                  :code :kotoba/internal-error :severity :error
                  :message "internal compiler error"}}
    {:file "wasm32-hetero-vector.kotoba" :target "wasm32-browser" :exit 70
     :code :kotoba/internal-error}
    {:file "hetero-vector-counterexample.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 0}
    {:file "src/h264/rbsp.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 0}
    {:file "src/h264/expgolomb.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 70
     :code :kotoba/internal-error}
    {:file "hetero-vector-with-entry.kotoba" :target "wasm32" :exit 70
     :code :kotoba/internal-error
     :note "has a main entry, so the JVM path's entryless explanation does
            not apply, and the nbb path still crashes"}
    {:file "wasm32-hetero-vector.kotoba" :compiler "kbb -M:kotoba (amu 3579cfd, OLDER frontend)"
     :target "wasm32-browser" :exit 70
     :diagnostic {:error :target :code :kotoba/internal-error
                  :message "entryless libraries currently require the kotoba-script web target"
                  :details {:phase :target :target :wasm32-browser-kotoba-v1}}}
    ;; The uncoupled comparison. Same amu revision on both sides, and an
    ;; entry present so the entryless rule above cannot explain it.
    {:file "hetero-vector-with-entry.kotoba" :compiler "JVM, amu 02c7e57 as :local/root"
     :target "wasm32" :exit 0}
    {:file "hetero-vector-with-entry.kotoba" :compiler "bin/amu (nbb), amu 02c7e57"
     :target "wasm32" :exit 70 :code :kotoba/internal-error}]
   :second-finding
   "The nbb path COLLAPSES a structured diagnostic. On the same input the JVM
    compiler answers :error :target with a specific :message and a :details
    map naming the phase and target; bin/amu answers :error :internal,
    :message \"internal compiler error\", and no :details. Whatever the
    underlying defect turns out to be, an operator on the JDK-free front door
    currently cannot see the reason at all, and the temptation is to read
    'internal compiler error' as one cause when the two paths are refusing
    for different ones."
   :confirmed
   "At MATCHED amu revisions and with an entry present, the JVM compiler
    compiles this exit 0 and bin/amu exits 70 with :kotoba/internal-error. So
    it is a defect on the JDK-free (nbb) entrypoint specifically, not a
    property of the language or of the version skew."
   :finding "The trigger is `hetero-vector-at`, not the [:vector [:i64 :i64]]
             tuple type: constructing one and returning it inside a :result
             compiles cleanly, and reading one element out of it crashes the
             compiler. `amu check` passes the crashing file, so the failure is
             in lowering, not admission. This is why expgolomb.kotoba -- whose
             every result is read with hetero-vector-at -- has no wasm32
             artifact, while quant.kotoba and transform.kotoba do."
   :status :open-blocker-for-expgolomb-on-wasm}

  {:id :native-export-table-rejected
   :file "native-export-table-rejected.kotoba"
   :also "src/h264/quant.kotoba, of which it is a byte-for-byte copy"
   :measured
   [{:command ["compile" "--target" "aarch64"] :exit 0}
    {:command ["extract-native" "--symbol" "group-idx"] :exit 65
     :diagnostic {:format :kotoba.cli-error/v1 :ok false :error :verify
                  :code :kotoba/verification-failed :severity :error
                  :message "native export table rejected"}}
    {:note "all six exports reject identically -- group-idx, level-scale,
            ac-qmul, dc-qmul, dequant-ac, chroma-qp -- so it is the artifact,
            not one symbol"}
    {:file "src/h264/transform.kotoba" :command ["extract-native"] :exit 0
     :note "compiled in the same run, on the same host; all four of its
            exports extract cleanly, e.g. idct4-1d at offset 116 length 232
            arity 5"}]
   :where "kotoba.verifier rejects when the artifact's stored export table
           differs from the one produced by re-running the emitter on the
           artifact's own KIR"
   :emission-is-not-the-variable
   "kotoba.native.aarch64/emit-program run twice on this module's KIR returns
    the identical export table both times, and likewise for transform. The
    disagreement is therefore between COMPILE-time and VERIFY-time emission,
    not between two verify-time emissions."
   :shrink-attempts-that-do-NOT-reproduce
   ["a self-recursive function (pow2) exported through a caller"
    "a six-arm cond chain"
    "one export calling another export"]
   :it-got-worse-during-this-session
   "At amu 02c7e57 only the quant module rejected; transform extracted cleanly
    (idct4-1d at offset 116 length 232 arity 5, idct4-1d-dc at 348,
    idct4-1d-scaled at 404) and its machine code was executed through the kexe
    loader over 8 real fixture blocks with 0 mismatches. amu then advanced to
    0df9d99 while this work was in progress, and at 0df9d99 the SAME
    transform.kotoba, compiled the same way, is ALSO rejected. So this is a
    regression across those two revisions, not a fixed property of either
    module -- and the module that still worked is the one whose native
    execution is the evidence in docs/measurement/jvm-free-residual-kernel.edn."
   :impact "The native path could execute the transform kernel at 02c7e57 and
            can execute neither at 0df9d99. The verifier reports each module
            separately, so a --native run today prints
            NATIVE-<isa>-transform UNVERIFIED and NATIVE-<isa>-quant
            UNVERIFIED with this diagnostic and exits 3. It is reported, not
            hidden, and not counted as a pass."
   :status :open-regression}

  {:id :require-clause-not-admitted
   :note "Already recorded in README.md under 'The guest-grammar reader does
          not pass amu check yet'. Re-measured 2026-08-30 unchanged: a guest
          namespace carrying a :require exits 65 with
          :kotoba.error/namespace-export-clause, so src/h264/sps.kotoba and
